<h1>Credential Verification</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Gate checks whether found credentials are still live by making read-only API calls to each provider.</p>

    <h2 id="what-verification-does">What Verification Does</h2>

    <p>When Gate finds a credential, it already knows the pattern matches. Verification answers the follow-up question: <em>is this credential still active?</em> Gate does this by making a single, read-only API call to the provider using the credential. The call does not modify anything -- it only asks "who am I?" or "is this token valid?".</p>

    <p>Verification results directly influence severity:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Live credential</strong> -- severity is upgraded to CRITICAL regardless of the rule's default severity. A live credential in source code is an active breach.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Inactive / revoked credential</strong> -- severity is downgraded to LOW. The credential is still worth removing, but poses no immediate risk.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Verification inconclusive</strong> -- original rule severity is preserved. This happens on network timeout, TLS error, or an unexpected response code.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Read-only, always.</strong> Every verification call Gate makes is a read-only operation. Gate never creates, modifies, or deletes anything at the provider. It never performs actions on your behalf.
    </div>

