    <h2 id="timeout-and-parallelism">Timeouts and Parallelism</h2>

    <p>Verification is designed to be fast enough for interactive use. Gate enforces:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Per-provider timeout:</strong> 2 seconds. If a provider does not respond within 2 seconds, the verification for that credential is marked inconclusive and the original severity is preserved.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Total budget:</strong> 5 seconds for all verification calls in a scan. If the budget is exhausted, remaining verifications are skipped.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Parallel execution:</strong> All provider calls are made concurrently, so a scan with findings across 5 providers takes ~2 seconds, not ~10.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="caching">Caching</h2>

    <p>Gate caches verification results to avoid redundant API calls across scans. Results are stored in <code>~/.gate/verify-cache.json</code>. Each entry is keyed by the sha256 hash of the credential value (never the plaintext value itself) and has a 1-hour TTL.</p>

    <p>This means:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If you scan the same repository twice within an hour, verification results from the first scan are reused.</li>
      <li>The cache file never contains plaintext credentials -- only hashes and the verification outcome.</li>
      <li>Stale entries are pruned automatically on each scan run.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># ~/.gate/verify-cache.json (excerpt, illustrative)</span>
{
  <span class="str">"a3f1c9..."</span>: { <span class="str">"status"</span>: <span class="val">"live"</span>, <span class="str">"provider"</span>: <span class="val">"stripe"</span>, <span class="str">"expires"</span>: <span class="val">1742000000</span> },
  <span class="str">"7d82e1..."</span>: { <span class="str">"status"</span>: <span class="val">"inactive"</span>, <span class="str">"provider"</span>: <span class="val">"github"</span>, <span class="str">"expires"</span>: <span class="val">1741996400</span> }
}</div>

    <h2 id="severity-impact">How Verification Affects Severity</h2>

    <p>Verification is the primary mechanism for severity adjustment at scan time. The outcome of each verification call feeds directly into the reported severity:</p>

