    <h2 id="enabling-verification">Enabling Verification</h2>

    <p>Verification is <strong style="color: var(--text-1);">off by default</strong> everywhere &mdash; scans, hooks, and CI. Gate makes no network calls with detected secrets unless you explicitly opt in, at one of three levels:</p>

    <h3>Dedicated command</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate verify</span></div>

    <h3>Per-scan flag</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --verify</span></div>

    <h3>.gaterc configuration</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># .gaterc</span>
<span class="kw">verify</span>: <span class="val">true</span></div>

    <p>As a hard off-switch, <code>GATE_OFFLINE=1</code> disables all outbound network activity regardless of flags or config. Use this in air-gapped environments or when network access from the scanner is prohibited by policy.</p>

    <h2 id="pre-commit-verification">Verification in Pre-Commit Hooks</h2>

    <p>Hooks follow the same opt-in rule. Beyond privacy, the reason is practical: a 2-second network call per credential in a commit hook adds noticeable latency to every commit, which degrades developer experience over time.</p>

    <p>To enable verification in pre-commit hooks, set <code>verify: true</code> in <code>.gaterc</code> and ensure the machine running the hook has network access. This is a reasonable tradeoff for high-security repositories where commit-time latency is acceptable.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>CI verification:</strong> CI is opt-in too. Pass <code>verify: true</code> to the GitHub Action (or add <code>--verify</code> to your scan step) to check findings against provider APIs during CI runs.
    </div>

    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy and Data Handling</h2>

    <p>Credential verification is designed with privacy as a hard constraint:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Credentials are sent only to their own provider.</strong> A GitHub token is sent only to <code>api.github.com</code>. A Stripe key is sent only to <code>api.stripe.com</code>. Gate does not relay credentials through any intermediary.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Gate's own servers are never involved.</strong> Verification calls are made directly from the local machine to the provider. No credential data is sent to PenumbraForge or any third party.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">The cache stores hashes, not values.</strong> Credentials are hashed with sha256 before being written to the cache. The plaintext value is never persisted to disk by Gate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="safety">Safety Guarantees</h2>

    <p>Every verification call Gate makes is read-only. Gate's verification code is structured to make this impossible to violate accidentally:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Only HTTP GET and safe POST endpoints (like <code>auth.test</code> or GraphQL <code>viewer</code> queries) are used.</li>
      <li>No write scopes are requested or exercised.</li>
      <li>Gate does not store session state, cookies, or authentication tokens from provider responses.</li>
      <li>Response bodies are parsed only for the status/active field; all other fields are discarded.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="tls">TLS Enforcement</h2>

    <p>All verification calls use HTTPS with strict TLS validation. Gate explicitly sets <code>rejectUnauthorized: true</code> in all HTTP clients. If a provider's TLS certificate cannot be validated (expired, self-signed, or hostname mismatch), Gate skips verification for that credential with a warning rather than proceeding over an insecure connection.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="warn">  warn  TLS validation failed for provider "supabase" — skipping verification</span>
<span class="dim">        credential severity unchanged (rule default: high)</span></div>

